  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Zhinghoua lang'Gleam spread the courier manifests across her counting-table and let the numbers be wrong again.
    
    Fourteen fire-cores logged outbound from the Nightshelf depot, winter of the founding's third cycle. Fourteen stamped, sealed, certified by a courier named Splitpath whose ink-marks still glowed faintly on the parchment's edge. Fourteen received at the Promenade Ward's heating infrastructure, entered into the ice-memory by whoever kept the founding-era records. But the heat-conductor array in the eastern quarter ran on a sixteen-core lattice—always had, since the first season. She'd certified the conductors herself, read their thermal history with her own scarred flippers, felt the crystalline signature of sixteen cores bedded into the ice like teeth in a jaw. Sixteen planted. Fourteen delivered. The numbers didn't balance.
    
    She pressed her left flipper flat against the ledger to stop its trembling and looked up at the dawn light cutting through the Promenade's crystal spires.
    
    Summit-Lord Yahailong was already on the promenade walkway, staff in hand, making her morning circuit of the heat-conductor stations. The aurora-glass glowed fitfully in her grip—flickering, always flickering, as though something inside it couldn't decide whether to stay lit. She paused at the eastern array, ran her flipper along the crystalline housing, and Zhinghoua watched her count the conductor nodes. Lips moving. One through sixteen. The same count she made every dawn, the same route she'd walked since the founding, and when she finished she turned west, away from the memorial wall where the names of the dead were carved, and continued without looking back.
    
    Hainou mewei'Frostglass arrived at the market stalls three minutes later, silver clasps catching the light, her pace unhurried and exact. She set a crate of fire-core samples on the merchant's block—Nightshelf stock, newly certified, the courier seal still tacky—and began arranging them by thermal grade with the practiced efficiency of someone who had never once miscounted a shipment. Her left flipper shook as she worked. She compensated by gripping each core a half-second longer than necessary, steadying it before placement, and if you weren't watching her hands you'd never notice. She'd poured fire-core tea that morning, Zhinghoua knew—the way she always did on certification days, two cups, one left cold for a student named Oeichowei who'd ascended to heights Hainou never would. A ritual of remembrance performed through routine. A woman who poured tea for ghosts would not miscalculate a shipment.
    
    "Zhinghoua." Hainou's voice carried the flat astronomical cadence of formal greeting. "You're counting again."
    
    "The eastern lattice."
    
    "Ah." Hainou set down a fire-core with deliberate care. "Yahailong oversaw that installation personally. She made the decisions that kept the Ward alive in those first winters. If there's a discrepancy, she would know."
    
    "I'm sure she would."
    
    Hainou's flipper trembled. She picked up another core. "I gave her everything she needed, back then. Tools, supply lines, access to the ice-records. Whatever the colony required." She paused, and her voice flattened further, as though she were reciting coordinates. "The alignment required sacrifice. We all understood that."
    
    Zhinghoua watched Hainou's hands and said nothing about the tremor.
    
    ---
    
    The trail led to Bastion Apex first, because that was where the ice-memory felt wrong.
    
    Zhinghoua descended through the crystal passages with a fire-core lantern and her certification tools, reading the thermal residue layered into the walls. Ice-memory was supposed to be immutable—the Berg's own record, pressed into frozen stone by the weight of what happened above it. You could read a wall and feel the echo of a conversation held forty years ago, taste the mineral signature of a fire-core that had burned in a specific brazier on a specific night. It was the only archive that couldn't be falsified.
    
    Except the ice near Bastion Apex tasted like anesthesia.
    
    She pressed her scarred flippers to the wall and felt it: a creeping numbness that started at the sternum and spread outward, a cold that wasn't temperature but *absence*. Snowdrift-numb residue. Someone had walked through this ice in the meditative state, reading—or rewriting—the impressions stored within it. The residue was old. Founding-era old.
    
    Hainou mewei'Frostglass had discovered Snowdrift-numb here. Had found the Dreaming Tooth in these passages, the relic that first radiated the technique. Her flipper bore the permanent tremor of someone who'd practiced the magic too often, too deep. And she never spoke of what she'd seen in the ice's memory when it witnessed her at Apex—just poured her cold tea on certification mornings with the same careful steadiness, the same refusal to look at what her hands were doing.
    
    Zhinghoua pulled her flipper from the wall and stared at the frost-crystals forming on her scarred skin.
    
    The certification records for the founding's third-cycle fire-core shipments had been logged into ice-memory by an Aurora Stack official with Snowdrift-numb access. The courier manifests said fourteen. The ice said fourteen. But the conductors said sixteen, and conductors didn't lie—they were physical objects bedded in physical ice, and you couldn't Snowdrift-numb a copper lattice into forgetting how many cores it held.
    
    Someone had altered the ice-memory to hide two fire-cores. And Hainou had the means, the access, and the tremor to prove she'd been deep in the technique during exactly the right period.
    
    Zhinghoua copied the residue signatures into her ledger with numb fingers and climbed back toward the light, already composing the formal inquiry she'd bring to the council. The numbers didn't balance. Hainou's hands shook. The logic was clean.
    
    It was clean for six days.
    
    ---
    
    The Shaled Scathe smelled like rendered fat and smoke-char, and Zhinghoua's beak ached before she'd passed the first fire-brazier. The refugee caverns stretched around her—uneven walls with tool-marks still visible, support pillars leaning wrong, names carved into the ice with bone-picks and claws. Aurora Stack families who'd lost spires. She tried not to read the names.
    
    She was here for the shadow-ledgers. Nightshelf records, kept in ink on hide rather than in ice-memory, because the Nightshelf had never trusted the aurora's frozen archive. Courier logs. Manifests. The independent record that existed outside the ice entirely.
    
    Keth'vex met her at the garrison checkpoint—the Nightshelf engineer who'd held her hand in the dark during the tunnel collapse, who still brought her fire-core samples twice a month. They didn't speak. Keth'vex handed her the founding-era courier binders and a cup of fire-core tea and sat beside her while she read.
    
    The shadow-ledgers confirmed fourteen cores shipped in the third cycle. But they also recorded something the ice-memory didn't: a second shipment, two cores, routed through a different courier three days later. Requisitioned by Summit-Lord Yahailong personally. Logged into the Nightshelf depot records with her seal. Delivered to the Promenade Ward's eastern quarter—completing the sixteen-core lattice.
    
    Hainou mewei'Frostglass had been at the Nightshelf trade summit during those three days. Her travel logs, preserved in courier ink that glowed in Zhinghoua's lantern-light, placed her two days' journey from Bastion Apex when the ice-memory was altered.
    
    Zhinghoua's left flipper trembled so badly she knocked the tea from the table. Keth'vex caught it. Held her hand.
    
    She stared at the courier logs and tried to think clearly, but the ice she'd read at Bastion Apex was still in her—the Snowdrift-numb residue had seeped into her own thermal signature, and she couldn't tell anymore whether the memory of fourteen cores was something she'd discovered or something the corrupted ice had told her. Her own mind tasted like anesthesia.
    
    The second shipment had been requisitioned to replace two cores Yahailong had diverted. To where, the shadow-ledger didn't say. But the memorial wall at the Promenade listed nine settlers who'd Walked during the founding's third winter—nine penguins who'd wandered onto the ice and never returned, their loss attributed to grief and cold and the Long Dark's pull. The Shaled Scathe memorial, carved by different hands in a different archive, listed eleven.
    
    Two names the Promenade ice didn't remember. Two fire-cores the Promenade ice didn't count. Zhinghoua pressed her forehead to the cold hide of the ledger and breathed until her heartbeat steadied.
    
    The numbers didn't balance because someone had made the ice forget.
    
    She thought of Hainou that morning, pouring cold tea for Oeichowei. A woman who kept faith with the dead through small, steady rituals—who carried her guilt not by erasing it but by performing it, every certification day, in the tremor of her flipper and the untouched cup. Hainou hadn't altered the ice. She'd taught someone else the technique, and then she'd spent every morning since arranging fire-cores with impossible precision, answering questions no one had asked, her shame manifesting as a supply chain so perfect it left nothing to examine except the one thing she'd never mention first.
    
    ---
    
    She found Yahailong at dawn, where she always was: walking the eastern heat-conductor array, counting the nodes, her aurora-glass staff flickering in her grip. One through sixteen. The same route. The same count. Numb flippers on a warm day.
    
    Zhinghoua set the courier logs on the conductor housing between them. Yahailong looked at them for a long time. Her hands didn't shake. They were past shaking—the nerve damage from years of Snowdrift-numb practice had settled into a permanent stillness that read as composure if you didn't know what caused it.
    
    "Two cores," Zhinghoua said. "Diverted from the third-cycle shipment. You replaced them three days later through a private requisition, but the two families in the eastern quarter had already gone three nights without heat. Moqai and Xuelin. They Walked." She swallowed. "Except they didn't Walk. They froze. And you entered the ice and made it forget their names."
    
    Yahailong's staff flickered. She looked west, toward the memorial she never visited—the one that listed nine, not eleven.
    
    "The colony orbits the light," she said quietly. "And sometimes the orbit requires—"
    
    "Don't."
    
    Silence. A courier passed behind them carrying a sealed crate, boots crunching past the walkway. Yahailong watched the courier go.
    
    "Hainou found the technique," Yahailong said. Her voice cracked into something rawer, and she stopped. Breathed. Resumed. "I asked her to teach me. She understood what it would be used for. She has carried that since the founding." A pause. "The two cores went to the western perimeter. The orca scouts had been sighted. I needed heat-barriers on the outer wall or we would have lost the entire Ward, not two families. I chose." Her flippers, perfectly still, rested on the conductor housing. "The ice remembered what I did. So I made it stop."
    
    "You can't actually make it stop," Zhinghoua said. The words came out steady, though her flipper shook. "I read the residue at Bastion Apex. Both versions are still there—the truth and your revision, layered like bad crystal. You didn't erase anything. You just made it harder to read clearly." She touched her own temple. "I can't tell anymore what I've discovered from what the corrupted ice told me. That's the cost—not just for you. For anyone who touches it."
    
    Yahailong turned from the memorial she wouldn't visit and looked at Zhinghoua with the expression of someone who had been paying for decades and was still paying. Her staff's glow guttered, almost died, held.
    
    "I made the decisions that kept the Ward alive," she said, and for the first time Zhinghoua heard what the sentence had always meant. Not pride. Confession. Every morning, walking the array she'd bought with two families and a silence carved into ice. Counting the sixteen cores. Remembering the two she'd stolen to place them.
    
    "The Accord," Zhinghoua said. "If the Nightshelf learns founding-era allocations were falsified—"
    
    "Yes."
    
    "The current projections. If past reserves were miscounted, the shortage calculations are wrong. Penguins will die."
    
    "Yes."
    
    The aurora pulsed overhead, green and blue and merciless. Zhinghoua thought of Hainou arranging fire-cores with trembling precision. Of Keth'vex catching a cup of tea in the dark. Of the Shaled Scathe's memorial wall, carved by different hands, holding every name the Promenade had been taught to forget.
    
    "The ice keeps better books than we do," Zhinghoua said. "That's not a curse. That's what stops us from becoming what we fear."
    
    Yahailong said nothing. Her staff flickered in her grip—the aurora-glass guttering, steadying, guttering again, the way it had every dawn since the founding, as though the light itself couldn't decide whether to stay.
    
    Zhinghoua gathered the courier logs. Her left flipper shook. She didn't hide it.
    
    Behind them, the memorial wall held nine names in the ice, and the cold held two more that the ice had been taught to forget, and somewhere in the Shaled Scathe a different wall remembered them all. Tomorrow she would bring the ledgers to council. Tomorrow the numbers would have to balance. But tonight, walking back through the Promenade with corrupted ice still seeping through her thermal signature, she stopped at Hainou's stall and poured the cold tea that sat untouched on the certification block—poured it out onto the ice, where it froze in a thin dark line, and let the Berg remember that too.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771589801296_0", anchor: "The orca scouts had been sighted", text: "Yahailong's justification rests entirely on this claim, and the chronicle accepts it without scrutiny. Orca scouting patterns during the founding era are poorly documented — I have noted elsewhere that at least two celebrated raid defenses from this period were retaliatory strikes provoked by penguin encroachment on hunting grounds. Whether these scouts were reconnoitering an attack or responding to the Ward's expansion into their territory would change the moral calculus considerably. Yahailong's framing — the whole Ward or two families — is the framing of someone who has already chosen and needs the choice to have been inevitable. I have used the same framing in my own reports. I recognized it immediately.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589801296_1", anchor: "I needed heat-barriers on the outer wall or we would have lost the entire Ward, not two families", text: "The reader should understand what a heat-barrier on the outer wall implies tactically. Orca raids are coordinated assaults: shore-breakers drive channels through weakened ice, herders push groups toward killing zones, finishers wait below escape routes. Heat-barriers maintain ice integrity against the shore-breaking phase. The tactical reasoning is sound. Whether the threat materialized is a different question the chronicle does not ask.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589801296_2", anchor: "nine settlers who'd Walked during the founding's third winter", text: "Walked. The Walking is a specific phenomenon — a compulsion seizing the grief-stricken during the Long Dark, distinguished from death-by-exposure precisely because no one is certain what it is. Moqai and Xuelin's families did not Walk. They froze because their heat was requisitioned. Logging them as Walked is not euphemism; it is a falsification of cause of death that allowed the colony to mourn them as victims of the Dark rather than of allocation. Yahailong did not just erase names from the memorial. She reclassified a policy decision as a force of nature. I have made smaller versions of the same error in my own cataloguing — writing 'ice-shift loss' when the loss had a signature and a seal.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589801296_3", anchor: "the Nightshelf had never trusted the aurora's frozen archive", text: "And here is why. Ink on hide, outside the ice entirely — the only reason the truth survives. Nightshelf record-keeping operates on the principle that any archive alterable by magic is not an archive. They are correct, and I say this as someone whose career is the aurora's frozen archive.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589801296_4", anchor: "the ice near Bastion Apex tasted like anesthesia", text: "Zhinghoua reads this as founding-era Snowdrift-numb residue. She is not wrong. But identical anesthetic signatures exist in Foundation Depths strata predating penguin settlement by geological ages. Whatever Hainou formalized at Bastion Apex, she found it because something was already numb there. The technique was not invented. It was remembered.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589801296_5", anchor: "Tomorrow she would bring the ledgers to council", text: "Which council? Aurora Stack governance has fractured — the Pinnacle, the Threshold, what remains of Qingfu'spire — into competing power centers that would weaponize this scandal before the ink dried. Bringing evidence to 'council' now is not presenting findings to a unified authority. It is arming every faction simultaneously. Zhinghoua is a merchant, not a politician. I recognize the optimism.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589801296_6", anchor: "poured it out onto the ice, where it froze in a thin dark line, and let the Berg remember that too", text: "A small kindness for the dead, recorded in the only medium that cannot be requisitioned or reclassified. I was not expecting this chronicle to end by undoing me over spilled tea. Professional failing.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
